data.content:
Your codebase is already doing exactly the right thing:

- ✅ **`System.IO.Abstractions`** is imported
- ✅ **`IFileSystem`** is injected via primary constructor
- ✅ File operations go through `fileSystem.File`

**No custom wrapper is needed.** `System.IO.Abstractions` provides the battle-tested `IFileSystem` interface, the `FileSystem` default implementation, and `MockFileSystem` for tests.

Just make sure your DI and test packages are set up:

```csharp
// DI registration (Program.cs / Startup.cs):
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

```bash
# Test project — install the testing helpers:
dotnet add <TestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

```csharp
// In tests, use MockFileSystem:
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/data.txt", new MockFileData("hello") }
});
var svc = new DataService(mockFs);
Assert.Equal("hello", svc.ReadData("/data.txt"));
```

You're all set — no wrapper generation required here.
